现在，我们专为你制作了这档终极《办公室》重温播客。

164
00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:04.580
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.
每周，我们都会深度解析一集《办公室》，并分享只有身临其境的我们才能告诉你的独家幕后故事。

165
00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:05.760
We're The Office Ladies.
我们是《办公室女士》。

166
00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:09.660
Hey, everybody.
嘿，大家好。

167
00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:15.880
So, if you see a photo of us podcasting, I am now in my husband's closet.
所以，如果你看到我们录制播客的照片，我现在是在我丈夫的衣帽间里。

168
00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:18.080
Those are not my baseball hats.
那些不是我的棒球帽。

169
00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:19.780
That is his baseball hat collection.
那是他的棒球帽收藏。

170
00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:21.500
I like to wear baseball hats.
我喜欢戴棒球帽。

171
00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:22.300
You do.
你确实喜欢。

172
00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:24.820
You are sort of like a baseball hat family.
你们家算是棒球帽家族吧。

173
00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:27.780
I feel like all of your family loves baseball hats.
我觉得你们全家都爱戴棒球帽。

174
00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:33.700
Oh, lady, one Christmas, we were trying to find a family photo that we could put on our Christmas card.
噢，亲爱的，有一年圣诞节，我们想找一张全家福印在圣诞贺卡上。

175
00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:37.300
And I couldn't find one where one of us wasn't wearing a hat.
结果我一张都找不到，因为照片里总有人戴着帽子。

176
00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:40.560
And so, we decided to make it a bit.
于是，我们决定把它变成一个梗。

177
00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:44.740
And we did a collage of just a baseball hat Christmas card.
我们做了一个全是棒球帽的拼贴画圣诞贺卡。

178
00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:47.460
My mother-in-law was mortified.
我婆婆当时尴尬坏了。

179
00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:48.900
Mortified.
尴尬透了。

180
00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:51.160
She's like, why are you all in hats?
她说，你们为什么都戴着帽子？

181
00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:55.080
Yes, baseball hats for your formal Christmas card.
没错，戴着棒球帽拍正式的圣诞贺卡。

182
00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:57.360
But, yeah, it was like an inside joke.
不过，那就像是我们内部的一个笑话。

183
00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:58.380
We're a hat family.
我们就是个帽子家族。

184
00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:02.100
We would fit in very well in Josh's closet, I guess is what I'm saying.
我想说的是，我们应该会很适合待在乔什的衣帽间里。

185
00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:03.860
I think you would.
我想也是。

186
00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:07.420
By the way, side note, your Christmas cards are always awesome.
顺便提一下，你们的圣诞贺卡总是很棒。

187
00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:13.840
My favorite, and I have it hanging up in the kitchen still, is the one where you guys are all holding Sunny, your cat.
我最喜欢的一张，现在还挂在我厨房里，就是你们全家抱着你们的猫桑尼的那张。

188
00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:15.140
Oh, yes.
噢，对。

189
00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:17.180
That was another fun collage.
那又是另一张有趣的拼贴画。

190
00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.040
We had a good time with that.
我们当时玩得很开心。

191
00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:20.800
Lee has Sunny on his head.
李把桑尼顶在头上。

192
00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.880
I'm holding Sunny upside down.
我把桑尼倒着抱。

193
00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:24.480
It was a good one.
那张拍得真好。

194
00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:25.320
Thanks, lady.
谢谢你，亲爱的。

195
00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:27.200
You guys have a floppy cat.
你们养的是布偶猫吧。

196
00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:29.300
And floppy cats are so great in photos.
布偶猫拍照效果太棒了。

197
00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:32.640
My cats would have, like, shredded our heads, like, if we tried that.
如果我试着那样抱我的猫，它们估计会把我们的头抓烂。

198
00:08:33.560 --> 00:08:34.380
Okay, okay.
好吧，好吧。

199
00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:35.680
So, here we go.
那么，我们开始吧。

200
00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:37.200
What are we doing today, Jenna?
珍娜，我们今天要聊什么？

201
00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:42.700
Today, we are talking about Season 3, Episode 12, Traveling Salesman.
今天我们要聊的是第三季第十二集《旅行推销员》。

202
00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:44.260
Oh, it's so good.
噢，这集太棒了。

203
00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:45.920
It's so good.
真的太棒了。

204
00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:49.500
I re-watched this one and The Return with my kids.
我和孩子们重温了这一集和《回归》那一集。

205
00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:51.360
We all snuggled up in bed and watched them.
我们挤在床上一起看了它们。

206
00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:53.620
They were laughing hysterically.
他们笑得前仰后合。

207
00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:56.780
Well, Angela, it's a good thing you watched those together.
安吉拉，你们一起看真是太好了。

208
00:08:56.780 --> 00:09:03.980
I did, too, because they were actually created to be kind of one big episode to be watched together.
我也这么觉得，因为这两集本来就是作为一整集来创作的，适合连在一起看。

209
00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:11.720
And that is why they were written by three writers, Mike Schur, Lee Eisenberg, and Gene Stupnitsky.
这就是为什么它们由三位编剧共同撰写：迈克·舒尔、李·艾森伯格和吉恩·斯图普尼茨基。

210
00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:15.480
And both of those episodes were directed by Greg Daniels.
而且这两集都是由格雷格·丹尼尔斯执导的。

211
00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:16.240
All right, Jenna.
好了，珍娜。

212
00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:17.620
Well, why don't you hit us with a summary?
要不你来给我们做个剧情梗概？

213
00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:18.360
All right.
好的。

214
00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:26.780
So, in Traveling Salesman, because that's the episode we're covering today, Dwight covers for Angela after she misses an important deadline.
在《旅行推销员》这一集中，也就是我们今天要聊的这一集，德怀特在安吉拉错过一个重要截止日期后替她顶了包。

215
00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:38.240
The sales team all pair up for sales calls, but at the same time, Angela uncharacteristically invites Pam to coffee and then discusses her love life.
销售团队两两配对去跑销售，但与此同时，安吉拉一反常态地约帕姆喝咖啡，并谈论了自己的感情生活。

216
00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:42.060
In code, but she discusses her love life.
虽然是用暗语说的，但她确实谈论了自己的感情生活。

217
00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:49.940
And then, upon the return of the sales team, Michael learns that Dwight went to corporate behind his back.
后来，当销售团队回来时，迈克尔发现德怀特背着他去找了公司高层。

218
00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:51.360
He doesn't know why.
他不知道原因。

219
00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:54.840
Dwight quits Dunder Mifflin.
德怀特辞职离开了邓德·米夫林。

220
00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:56.140
Yes!
没错！

221
00:09:56.440 --> 00:10:00.400
And then Karen finds out that Jim once had a thing for Pam.
然后凯伦发现吉姆曾经对帕姆有意思。

222
00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:02.600
That's a lot of stuff.
这信息量真大。

223
00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:04.400
That's a lot of stuff.
确实信息量很大。

224
00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:05.300
Phyllis, man.
菲利斯这家伙。

225
00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:06.420
She spilled the beans.
她把秘密给泄露了。

226
00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:07.660
I know.
我知道。

227
00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:08.680
Phyllis.
菲利斯啊。

228
00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:09.840
Phyllis.
菲利斯。

229
00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:11.020
Okay, we'll get to that.
好吧，我们之后会聊到这个。

230
00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:11.840
We'll get to that.
我们之后会聊到这个。

231
00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:14.620
I bet you have some really good fast facts.
我敢说你一定准备了一些很棒的速览小知识。

232
00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:18.180
Well, you know I do because you know that we got something.
你知道我肯定有，因为你知道我们准备了些东西。

233
00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:19.560
We did.
我们确实准备了。

234
00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:20.480
All right.
好的。

235
00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:22.960
So, here's what we got.
那么，这就是我们准备的内容。

236
00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:23.720
All right.
好的。

237
00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:24.900
Fast fact number one.
速览小知识第一条。

238
00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:30.980
Like I said, both this and the next episode, The Return, were written and treated like one big episode.
正如我所说，这一集和下一集《回归》都是作为一整集来撰写和处理的。

239
00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:33.680
They were both directed by Greg Daniels.
它们都是由格雷格·丹尼尔斯执导的。

240
00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:35.480
What did we do, Ange?
我们做了什么，安吉？

241
00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:36.840
Tell everybody what we did.
告诉大家我们做了什么。

242
00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:41.360
We reached out to Greg and he sent in audio clips.
我们联系了格雷格，他发来了音频片段。

243
00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:42.520
And they are fantastic.
而且它们棒极了。

244
00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:46.200
And now Jen and I want him to send in audio clips every single week.
现在我和珍都希望他每周都能发音频片段过来。

245
00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:47.240
Yes!
没错！

246
00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:49.600
His audio clips are so good, you guys.
伙计们，他的音频片段真的太棒了。

247
00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:50.680
We're giddy about them.
我们为此感到兴奋不已。

248
00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:55.380
So, the first thing we asked him was, what was the inspiration for this episode?
所以，我们问他的第一个问题是，这一集的灵感来源是什么？

249
00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:57.060
And here's what he had to say.
以下是他对此的回应。

250
00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:06.620
Traveling Salesman and The Return is a little two-episode arc that we did as part of the working out of the merger of the two branches stories.
《旅行推销员》和《回归》是我们为了处理两家分公司合并的故事线而制作的一个两集小篇章。

251
00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:12.520
Andy was always conceived in Gay Witch Hunt to be the Stanford version of Dwight.
在《同性恋猎手》一集中，安迪最初的设定就是斯坦福分部的德怀特。

252
00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:16.340
So, it was natural that they butt heads when the branches merged.
所以，当两家分部合并时，他们发生冲突是很自然的事。

253
00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:25.640
And we liked the idea that Dwight, as the hometown favorite, gets to have a really strong and romantic arc here.
我们很喜欢这样一个点子：德怀特作为家乡的宠儿，在这里能有一段非常深刻且浪漫的剧情线。

254
00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:29.220
You see a very noble side of him protecting the woman that he loves.
你会看到他非常高尚的一面，他在保护自己心爱的女人。

255
00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:40.560
And I think one of the reasons we made it two episodes was it didn't feel real to me or earned to have him leave and come back in only one episode.
我想我们把它拍成两集的原因之一是，如果只用一集让他离开又回来，我觉得不够真实，也不够有说服力。

256
00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:44.300
So, it just felt a lot better to spread it out over two.
所以，分摊到两集里效果要好得多。

257
00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:51.860
And another part about that storyline was that it was pairings.
关于那条故事线的另一个重点是角色配对。

258
00:11:52.260 --> 00:12:01.620
And, you know, a writing exercise that I often did with the writers was I'd have them try and come up with pairings of characters that we hadn't had scenes together before.
你知道，我经常和编剧们做的一个练习就是，让他们尝试想出一些以前从未有过对手戏的角色组合。

259
00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:08.820
And so, this entire episode was fun pairings like Ryan and Stanley or Phyllis and Karen.
所以，这一整集里都是有趣的配对，比如瑞安和斯坦利，或者菲利斯和凯伦。

260
00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:20.540
And, of course, Angela and Pam, who have a great emotional storyline where they really are kind of growing as friends.
当然，还有安吉拉和帕姆，她们有一条很棒的情感线，两人作为朋友真的在成长。

261
00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:25.780
And it was really cool to see Jim and Dwight, too, and see their backstory.
看到吉姆和德怀特，了解他们的背景故事，也真的很酷。

262
00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:35.140
And then the other thing about Traveling Salesman was we used in the client, we used being good at sales to make Michael more likable.
关于《旅行推销员》这一集，另一件事是我们在《客户》一集中，利用擅长销售这一点让迈克尔变得更讨人喜欢。

263
00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:41.480
And, you know, in Traveling Salesman, you see a whole bunch of different characters and how they are as salespeople.
你知道，在《旅行推销员》里，你会看到许多不同的角色，以及他们作为推销员的表现。

264
00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:52.560
And you see how great Jim and Dwight work together and Michael being like a straight man for Andy, who's just terrible.
你会看到吉姆和德怀特合作得有多棒，而迈克尔就像是安迪的捧哏，而安迪表现得简直糟糕透顶。

265
00:12:54.560 --> 00:13:07.520
And so, it kind of helps distinguish the more likable original Dwight versus the more sinister, schemey, you know, new Dwight.
这有助于区分那个更讨人喜欢的原版德怀特，和那个更阴险、更爱算计的新版德怀特。

266
00:13:07.900 --> 00:13:08.900
Keep talking.
继续说。

267
00:13:09.140 --> 00:13:10.260
Talk forever, Greg.
永远说下去吧，格雷格。

268
00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:11.920
Greg, talk forever.
格雷格，一直说下去。

269
00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:13.620
It's so fascinating.
这太迷人了。

270
00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:19.220
I love that not only were there these new pairings of characters, but also of writers.
我喜欢这一点，不仅有这些新的角色配对，还有编剧的配对。

271
00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:21.360
It's just so interesting.
这真的太有趣了。

272
00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:22.380
Yes.
是的。

273
00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:24.220
Yes, Ange.
没错，安吉。

274
00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:31.240
And so much comes out in this episode when you put these different people together for the first time, really.
当把这些不同的人第一次聚在一起时，这一集真的迸发出了很多东西。

275
00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:32.680
Yeah.
是啊。

276
00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:44.020
So, you guys, I don't know if we need to start some sort of a petition, but we really want to create a new feature of the show called Inspiration with Greg.
所以，伙计们，我不知道我们是否需要发起某种请愿，但我们真的很想在节目中开创一个新栏目，叫“格雷格的灵感时刻”。

277
00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:49.480
And he will send us an audio clip of the inspiration behind every episode.
他会给我们发一段音频，讲述每一集背后的灵感来源。

278
00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:50.820
And there's, like, piano music.
而且还要有那种钢琴背景音乐。

279
00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:57.240
Greg will tell us the inspiration, and then Kent tells us the nuts and bolts of how he made that inspiration happen.
格雷格告诉我们灵感，然后肯特告诉我们他是如何把这些灵感落到实处的细节。

280
00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:01.220
Greg is listening right now, and is like, ladies, what are you signing me up for?
格雷格现在正在听，心想：女士们，你们这是给我安排了什么活儿？

281
00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:02.100
Exactly.
没错。

282
00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:04.400
What is Fast Fact No.
第二个速记事实是什么？

283
00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:04.800
2?
2？

284
00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:10.060
Well, Angela, speaking of Kentopedia, Fast Fact No.
嗯，安吉拉，说到“肯特百科”，第二个速记事实叫“地点，地点，还是地点”。

285
00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:13.220
2 is called Locations, Locations, Locations.
2。

286
00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:22.600
So, for this episode, because we actually shot it in conjunction with The Return, we did something called crossboarding.
所以，对于这一集，因为我们实际上是和《回归》那一集一起拍摄的，我们做了一种叫“交叉拍摄”的操作。

287
00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:26.660
It's a little lingo that they use in the biz.
这是业内使用的一点行话。

288
00:14:31.900 --> 00:14:35.500
So, crossboarding is when you shoot two episodes at once.
交叉拍摄就是同时拍摄两集内容。

289
00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:46.780
We normally shot one episode each week, but we shot these episodes over a 10-day period from October 16, 2006 to October 27, 2006.
我们通常每周拍一集，但这两集我们是在2006年10月16日到2006年10月27日这10天内拍完的。

290
00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:51.680
And the main reason for this was because of locations.
主要原因就是因为拍摄地点。

291
00:14:53.020 --> 00:15:00.500
We just had a lot of places to go in these two episodes, and having the flexibility to shoot it over a two-week period was really helpful.
这两集里我们要去的地方太多了，能有两周时间的灵活性来拍摄真的很有帮助。

292
00:15:00.860 --> 00:15:03.080
Yes, and they would also do a thing, you guys.
是的，而且他们还会做一件事，伙计们。

293
00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:05.120
They call it shooting out a location.
他们称之为“拍完一个地点”。

294
00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:11.680
So, for example, every scene that happened in that coffee shop, they would film in one day.
比如，发生在咖啡店里的每一场戏，他们都会在一天内拍完。

295
00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:13.700
And then they could close out that location.
然后他们就可以结束那个地点的拍摄了。

296
00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:16.260
They don't have to pay for that location for multiple days.
他们就不必为那个地点支付多天的费用。

297
00:15:16.260 --> 00:15:22.420
So, sometimes when you crossboard, it's a great way to save money because you can shoot out a location.
